wishnode.net — Records Desk page content phext (phexts/records-desk.phext). Source of truth for the Records Desk page. Task 34.6.6/7.7.10/7.6.10. See docs/COORDINATE-MAP.md.Records Desk — Exocortexia | Phext, Inc.Records DeskPublic-records search and redaction · Neb. Rev. Stat. § 84-712 et seq.SUMMARY (plain English — rendered first)Records Desk helps staff answer requests for public records. You search the files, black out the few parts the law lets you hide, and send the rest with a clear bill. It is for Nebraska offices that answer records requests under state law.Click the button below to open the live demo. Type a word in the search box and press Search. Then open a file from the list. You see just what the public sees: the parts the law protects stay blacked out, and the rest reads in full.This demo is open to all, and it uses made-up data. No sign-in is needed, and no real files are here. In a real setup for your office, staff sign in first through edit.phext.io, and the files are your own real ones.What it doesA staff member searches the records, marks the parts that must stay hidden, and sends the rest to the person who asked.The demo holds about 400 made-up sample documents. The storage beneath it was also tested with 100,000 documents, and it stayed fast.Hiding text the right wayBlacking out text never changes the original. The hidden part is stored apart, as its own note. Staff with the right role can see the original next to the public copy and check each choice.You can only hide text for a reason the law lists. Nebraska law (§ 84-712.05) names the kinds of records that may be held back. The form offers just those kinds. A mark with no valid reason is refused.Each mark is written into a tamper-proof record — every entry is locked to the one before it, so if anyone changes a past entry, the change shows. The log notes who made the mark, why, and where.A fair bill, in plain sightThe reply packet shows its math. Pages come from the size of the files. Time comes from the page count. Cost comes from a stated pay rate. Anyone can check each line.This is not a made-up problem. In October 2026, Nebraska's health department told a man that copies of its emails would cost $976,263.77 — and would arrive in the year 2076. He has sued under the state's public-records law. Search and math do not take years. This demo shows the same job done in the open.What the law saysNebraska law gives each person the right to see public records in office hours.The office must reply at once, as a rule in four business days. If it needs more time, it must say so in writing and give a date.The office may charge just the true costs the law allows.Some records may be held back. The law (§ 84-712.05) lists the kinds. Each hidden part in this tool must name one of those kinds.Full statute sheet: see the Records Desk part of the statutes phext on this site. The page link reads “How Records Desk matches Nebraska law.”Good to knowAll sample documents are made up. No real records and no real personal information are in the demo.The pay rate in the sample bill ($28.00 per hour) is a sample rate we set. It is not a rate set by law. Your office would set its own rate.The suite is checked by 84 automated tests. Five of them check this program, and they include the rule that hiding text never changes the original.Ask about a pilotAsk about a pilot for your office. Use the form on this site — the link says “Ask about a pilot.” Tell us which program you are interested in and what your office needs. When you send the form, you get a tracking code. Keep it. You can use it on this site to check the state of your request at any time. We read every request, and a person from Phext, Inc. replies. We will talk through what a small pilot would look like for your office. There is no cost to ask, and asking does not sign you up for anything.Links on this page (descriptive labels)Read the Records Desk source code (for programmers) — Source tree in the Exocortexia repositoryHow Records Desk matches Nebraska law — Statute sheet, also held in phexts/statutes.phextFOR TECHNICAL STAFF — not main copy. Component: apps/records-desk · Default port: 8801 · Tests: 4 in this app, 69 workspace-wide · Sign-in: edit.phext.io SSO when configured; open demo mode with a visible banner otherwise · Source: github.com/wbic16/Exocortexia/tree/exo/apps/records-desk · Statute sheet source: docs/records-desk-statute.md in the Exocortexia repo.Original page text before the plain-English rewrite (provenance):
